# Line endings.
#
# Deliberately NOT `* text=auto`. Every one of this repository's 988 tracked
# text files is stored with CRLF, so a blanket normalisation would rewrite all
# of them — 966 Unity assets included — in a single commit. That diff is
# unreviewable, and it would land on a working macOS checkout for no functional
# gain: CRLF is harmless to Node, to Unity, and to every .md and .json here.
#
# It is not harmless to a POSIX shell. A `#!/bin/bash` line ending in CR makes
# the kernel search for an interpreter named, literally, `/bin/bash<CR>`, and
# all the user is told is
#
#     bash: ./study: /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# which names the wrong file and does not mention line endings at all. So the
# rule below is scoped to exactly the files an interpreter has to parse, and
# nothing else is touched.

study                        text eol=lf
*.sh                         text eol=lf
*.py                         text eol=lf
Server/scripts/demo.js       text eol=lf
Server/scripts/mic-check.js  text eol=lf

# cmd.exe is the mirror case. A batch file with LF-only endings breaks
# multi-line `if (...)` blocks — and breaks them quietly, which is worse than
# the bash failure above. study.cmd is full of them.
*.cmd                        text eol=crlf
*.bat                        text eol=crlf

# Binary, marked explicitly. A contributor whose git has core.autocrlf=true —
# the default on Windows, and the setting that produced the mess above — must
# never be able to insert CR bytes into a PNG, a WAV, or a managed DLL.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.ico     binary
*.tga     binary
*.psd     binary
*.exr     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.pptx    binary
*.docx    binary
*.xlsx    binary
*.wav     binary
*.mp3     binary
*.mp4     binary
*.ttf     binary
*.otf     binary
*.fbx     binary
*.dll     binary
*.so      binary
*.dylib   binary
*.jar     binary
*.aar     binary
*.apk     binary
*.aab     binary
*.zip     binary
*.unitypackage binary
